> I very much disagree with what you wrote.

> In C++ programs you certainly want the four canonical
> functions defined (c'tor, d'tor, copy c'tor, assign'op) in
> most cases.

And you don't in C?  The only difference in C is that you have
to explicitly call them, rather than the compiler doing it
automatically.

> Then there are all the getter/setter that are often advocated.

I'd say that the general recommendation is the opposite: don't
use them.

In the end, if you're writing C, you define a struct, and a lot
of functions to manipulate it, and cross your fingers that no
one touches it otherwise.  In C++, you don't have to cross your
fingers, and in some cases (constructors, etc.) the client code
doesn't have to call the functions; the compiler does it
automatically.  So there's not much difference, an extra
private: or public: here or there in C++, and an extra
initializeX or destroyX here and there in C.

But that's only true to a point.  C++ has a number of additional
features (polymorphism, generic types) which sometimes allow you
to write a lot less code.

